    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p30-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that a student selected at random owns a phone from brand $A$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{20}$",
          "worked": "Of the 100 students, 45 own a brand $A$ phone.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A)&=\\frac{45}{100}\\\\P(A)&=\\frac{9}{20}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 15
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that a female student selected at random owns a phone from brand $A$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{20}$",
          "worked": "Of the 60 female students, 27 own a brand $A$ phone.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\mid F)&=\\frac{27}{60}\\\\P(A\\mid F)&=\\frac{9}{20}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 15
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Explain briefly how parts (a) and (b) show that, among the surveyed students, being female and owning a phone from brand $A$ are independent.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(A\\mid F)=P(A)$, so the two events are independent.",
          "worked": "Parts (a) and (b) both give $\\frac{9}{20}$. Since $P(A\\mid F)=P(A)$, restricting the selection to female students leaves the probability of owning a brand $A$ phone unchanged. The events are therefore independent for a random selection from this survey.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Does this automatically mean that, among the surveyed students, being male and owning a phone from brand $A$ are also independent? Verify your answer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. $P(A\\mid M)=\\frac{18}{40}=\\frac{9}{20}=P(A)$.",
          "worked": "Yes. The female and male rows account for all the students. The overall proportion is the weighted average of the two row proportions. If the female proportion equals the overall proportion, the male proportion must equal it too.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\mid M)&=\\frac{18}{40}\\\\P(A\\mid M)&=\\frac{9}{20}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis equals $P(A)$, verifying independence.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
